|> Exercise
    work=ing
    handle=_sine_rule_

    How to express the two sides marked ‘?’ as functions
    of $\alpha$, $\beta$, $\gamma$ and $a$? (NB: You can 
    trust the drawing's suggestion that $\beta > \eta$, $\gamma < \eta$, $\alpha < \eta$.)
    !! “trust”
    !! the drawing in the sense of $\beta$ being greater than
    !! $\eta$ and of $\gamma$, $\alpha$ being less than $\eta$.)

    |> Image
        src=tmp-images/e5_sine_rule_statement.svg

    |> Solution

        The bottom length of ‘?’ is

        $$
        a\cdot\sin(\gamma)\cdot\csc(\alpha)
        $$

        or

        $$
        a\cdot {\sin \gamma\over \sin\alpha}
        $$

        as illustrated here:

        |> Pause

        The top length of ‘?’, on the other hand,
        is 

        $$
        a\cdot\sin(\beta)\cdot\csc(\alpha)
        $$

        or

        $$
        a\cdot{\sin\beta\over \sin\alpha}
        $$

        per a similar approach, while also using the fact that
        $\sin(2\eta - x) = \sin(x)$ for all $x \in \rr$:

        |> SolutionNote

            One more generally has...

            |> Boxed

                $$
                \begin{gather*}
                {a\over b} = {\sin \alpha\over \sin\beta}\qquad{b\over a} = {\sin \beta\over \sin\alpha}            \\
                {a\over c} = {\sin \alpha\over \sin\gamma}\qquad{c\over a} = {\sin \gamma\over \sin\alpha} \up{2.2} \\
                {b\over c} = {\sin \beta\over \sin\gamma}\qquad{c\over a} = {\sin \gamma\over \sin\beta}   \up{2.2}
                \end{gather*}
                $$

            ...(it's $6$ times the same equation, no use
            looking at all of them) for a general triangle
            of sidelengths $a$, $b$, $c$ as labeled here...

            |> Pause

            ...though we can condense these equations to...

            |> Boxed
                $$
                \begin{gather*}
                {a\over \sin\alpha} = {b\over \sin\beta}            \\
                {a\over \sin\alpha} = {c\over \sin\gamma}  \up{2.2} \\
                {b\over \sin\gamma} = {c\over \sin\gamma}  \up{2.2}
                \end{gather*}
                $$

            ...or even to a single line...

            |> Boxed
                $$
                {a\over \sin\alpha} = {b\over \sin\beta} = {c\over \sin\gamma}
                $$

            ...through cross-multiplication/division!

        |> SolutionNote

            This result (e.g., the last boxed equation above)
            is known as the _sine rule_.

        |> SolutionNote

            Amusingly (but not that we really care) the
            same equations hold if we change the definitions
            of $\alpha$, $\beta$, and $\gamma$ to be the
            exterior angles of the triangle:

            |> Pause

            ...then each of $\sin \alpha$,
            $\sin \beta$, $\sin \gamma$ changes sign 
            _and only sign_ 
            !! (sine?),
            (
            |> del
                sine?
            \ sign),
            !! while keeping the same absolute value, 
            leaving the original
            equations intact.

        |> SolutionNote

            !! The solution technique of 
            Exercise >>_triangle_conch_
            uses a similar-if-not-identical solution technique.

            !! This exercise's solution approach already
            !! appeared, essentially, in the solution to
            !! Exercise >>_triangle_conch_. (If you recall.)

            !! The solution technique already appears, essentially, in the
            !! solution to Exercise >>_triangle_conch_.

            !! The solution approach that we used in this
            !! exercise already appeared, essentially, in the
            !! solution to Exercise >>_triangle_conch_, if you recall.
        